WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:04.780
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:00:04.791 --> 00:00:06.480
Good afternoon everyone.

00:00:06.481 --> 00:00:10.820
And welcome to the MSR AI
distinguished lecture series.

00:00:10.821 --> 00:00:14.225
I'm delighted today
to have Yoshua Bengio,

00:00:14.226 --> 00:00:15.910
from the University of Montreal,

00:00:15.911 --> 00:00:20.590
as our second in
a long series of speakers.

00:00:20.591 --> 00:00:24.080
Yoshua's immediately
recognizable as

00:00:24.081 --> 00:00:25.335
one of the key figures

00:00:25.336 --> 00:00:27.710
in the Deep Learning
revolution that's

00:00:27.711 --> 00:00:30.770
taken place in
the last five years.

00:00:30.771 --> 00:00:33.810
And so for those of you who
think he's new to the field,

00:00:33.811 --> 00:00:35.490
just jumped in this century,

00:00:35.491 --> 00:00:37.960
he's been at it for
more than 25 years.

00:00:37.961 --> 00:00:39.230
In fact, I wrote down one of

00:00:39.231 --> 00:00:41.310
his earliest papers was
something called data

00:00:41.311 --> 00:00:43.975
driven execution of
multi-layer networks

00:00:43.976 --> 00:00:45.840
for automatic
speech recognition,

00:00:45.841 --> 00:00:47.860
which was published in 1988,

00:00:47.861 --> 00:00:49.575
I believe at a AAAI.

00:00:49.576 --> 00:00:51.275
&gt;&gt; That's 30 years.

00:00:51.276 --> 00:00:56.070
&gt;&gt; Oh, you're right. I'm
not very good at math,

00:00:56.071 --> 00:00:57.665
sort of soft math.

00:00:57.666 --> 00:01:01.180
And many of the important
advances that we see

00:01:01.181 --> 00:01:04.720
today in speech, vision, text,

00:01:04.721 --> 00:01:07.665
and images, machine translation,

00:01:07.666 --> 00:01:09.250
are directly attributable to

00:01:09.251 --> 00:01:12.840
Yoshua's work and
that of his students.

00:01:12.841 --> 00:01:16.860
And that work, that recognition
is evident in many ways.

00:01:16.861 --> 00:01:19.190
If you start with
something like citations,

00:01:19.191 --> 00:01:22.625
his work has been
cited last year alone,

00:01:22.626 --> 00:01:25.060
more than 33,000 times,

00:01:25.061 --> 00:01:27.620
that's a career for
dozens of people

00:01:27.621 --> 00:01:31.715
and just a one year sample
of Yoshua's influence.

00:01:31.716 --> 00:01:34.820
The other way in which
his work is felt,

00:01:34.821 --> 00:01:36.595
is in the long stream of

00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:39.480
algorithmic innovations
that he's had.

00:01:39.481 --> 00:01:40.580
Most recently, they come in

00:01:40.581 --> 00:01:42.665
the form of
unsupervised learning,

00:01:42.666 --> 00:01:46.585
notably the work on
generative adversarial networks,

00:01:46.586 --> 00:01:48.600
on attention models,

00:01:48.601 --> 00:01:51.320
such as gating that's been
used for machine translation,

00:01:51.321 --> 00:01:53.430
but really opens up
whole other doors

00:01:53.431 --> 00:01:56.020
to using a variety of
other data structures.

00:01:56.021 --> 00:01:59.620
And then perhaps the one
that's a little more hidden,

00:01:59.621 --> 00:02:01.965
the form of influence
it's a little more hidden,

00:02:01.966 --> 00:02:04.255
is his tremendous work in

00:02:04.256 --> 00:02:07.030
education and in
supporting the community.

00:02:07.031 --> 00:02:08.170
It comes from his textbook,

00:02:08.171 --> 00:02:11.135
which is advertised
there, a lovely volume.

00:02:11.136 --> 00:02:13.115
But, you note that it also has

00:02:13.116 --> 00:02:15.020
all of the chapters online.

00:02:15.021 --> 00:02:17.480
He's worked tirelessly to

00:02:17.481 --> 00:02:19.960
promote new conferences
in the area,

00:02:19.961 --> 00:02:22.480
like ICLR, develop community,

00:02:22.481 --> 00:02:25.020
develop tools that are
broadly shared and to

00:02:25.021 --> 00:02:27.510
educate a whole new
generation of leaders,

00:02:27.511 --> 00:02:30.160
and I think that in
many ways may be as

00:02:30.161 --> 00:02:33.655
lasting a legacy as
all the technical contributions.

00:02:33.656 --> 00:02:35.770
And so today, what he's
going to talk about is

00:02:35.771 --> 00:02:37.945
how we move on from all of

00:02:37.946 --> 00:02:39.890
the amazing breakthroughs
that we've seen

00:02:39.891 --> 00:02:42.525
along perceptual dimensions
and speech vision,

00:02:42.526 --> 00:02:44.660
and to some extent language,

00:02:44.661 --> 00:02:46.880
to go to a much
Higher-level Cognition,

00:02:46.881 --> 00:02:49.885
which has been a pursuit that
he's been interested in for

00:02:49.886 --> 00:02:51.495
many years since the early

00:02:51.496 --> 00:02:53.590
PDP models and
perhaps before that.

00:02:53.591 --> 00:02:55.590
So please join me
in welcoming Yoshua.

00:02:55.591 --> 00:03:02.830
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Susan.
You hear me fine?

00:03:02.831 --> 00:03:03.355
Yeah?

00:03:03.356 --> 00:03:04.890
Okay.

00:03:04.891 --> 00:03:16.425
So. Yeah. You see me fine.

00:03:16.426 --> 00:03:21.140
So here, we have a little bug.

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:27.250
We're just going to.

00:03:27.251 --> 00:03:32.345
So, thanks Susan
for the kind words.

00:03:32.346 --> 00:03:34.720
And as you said,

00:03:34.721 --> 00:03:41.150
I'd like us to move away
from what we're doing now,

00:03:41.151 --> 00:03:43.730
which is great and is giving us

00:03:43.731 --> 00:03:46.960
amazing industrial successes to

00:03:46.961 --> 00:03:49.475
something closer
to human level AI.

00:03:49.476 --> 00:03:52.480
And in that respect,

00:03:52.481 --> 00:03:54.820
I think it's important
to look at the kinds

00:03:54.821 --> 00:03:57.930
of mistakes and failures that
our current systems have.

00:03:57.931 --> 00:04:01.480
And I spent some time
examining that.

00:04:01.481 --> 00:04:04.020
I'm sure most of
you are aware of

00:04:04.021 --> 00:04:07.130
the adversarial example's issue
illustrated here

00:04:07.131 --> 00:04:09.265
from the work of

00:04:09.266 --> 00:04:11.600
my former students
Ian Goodfellow and his

00:04:11.601 --> 00:04:14.550
collaborators at Google Brain,

00:04:14.551 --> 00:04:17.660
showing that if you just
change an image a little

00:04:17.661 --> 00:04:20.740
bit in a very
purposeful for a way,

00:04:20.741 --> 00:04:24.700
you can completely
fool a classifier,

00:04:24.701 --> 00:04:30.420
but, if we go beyond
this sort of amazing thing,

00:04:30.421 --> 00:04:32.510
what can we conclude

00:04:32.511 --> 00:04:35.350
about the failures of
our current systems?

00:04:35.351 --> 00:04:42.880
And I would say,
the strongest thing I see is,

00:04:42.881 --> 00:04:45.255
that they are learning in

00:04:45.256 --> 00:04:48.865
a way that exploits
superficial clues,

00:04:48.866 --> 00:04:52.710
that help to do the task
they're asked to do.

00:04:52.711 --> 00:04:56.150
But often, these are not

00:04:56.151 --> 00:04:57.580
the kind of clues that

00:04:57.581 --> 00:05:00.335
humans would consider to
be the most important,

00:05:00.336 --> 00:05:04.410
and often these
reveal that the models

00:05:04.411 --> 00:05:08.360
don't really capture
the underlying explanations,

00:05:08.361 --> 00:05:12.410
the underlying nature like
the objects for example,

00:05:12.411 --> 00:05:14.065
as we understand them,

00:05:14.066 --> 00:05:18.435
by thinking about images
as coming from physics,

00:05:18.436 --> 00:05:20.815
and in the 3-D world.

00:05:20.816 --> 00:05:24.080
So, a lot of

00:05:24.081 --> 00:05:27.625
what are we talking about
is how can we move forward

00:05:27.626 --> 00:05:32.220
beyond this tendency
of current models to

00:05:32.221 --> 00:05:33.470
sort of cheat by picking on

00:05:33.471 --> 00:05:37.180
surface regularities, and
why this is important.

00:05:37.181 --> 00:05:41.310
So we just put out
a couple of months ago,

00:05:41.311 --> 00:05:46.520
a paper illustrating one more
time one of these bailings.

00:05:46.521 --> 00:05:50.830
So we take Deep
Convolutional resonets

00:05:50.831 --> 00:05:56.880
and we change something

00:05:56.881 --> 00:05:58.730
superficial in
the data distribution,

00:05:58.731 --> 00:06:06.140
which is the spectral
distribution in fourier space.

00:06:06.141 --> 00:06:09.100
For example by, filtering in

00:06:09.101 --> 00:06:11.985
fourier space the images
we take, images like these,

00:06:11.986 --> 00:06:16.310
and in the fourier domain
we apply a mask like this,

00:06:16.311 --> 00:06:17.820
which just smooths things out.

00:06:17.821 --> 00:06:20.085
So in other words, we get
rid of high frequencies,

00:06:20.086 --> 00:06:23.085
and you basically
don't see much changed.

00:06:23.086 --> 00:06:26.790
But unfortunately, a network
train with these kinds of

00:06:26.791 --> 00:06:29.305
images does very poorly
on these kinds of images.

00:06:29.306 --> 00:06:32.830
And it gets even worse if you
do this kind of filtering,

00:06:32.831 --> 00:06:35.810
where you randomly enhance or

00:06:35.811 --> 00:06:39.105
reduce some of
the spatial frequencies,

00:06:39.106 --> 00:06:41.015
and now you get these images

00:06:41.016 --> 00:06:43.590
which humans would still
recognize properly,

00:06:43.591 --> 00:06:45.710
but there some weird colors that

00:06:45.711 --> 00:06:48.615
show up in some
places and so on.

00:06:48.616 --> 00:06:51.645
And that completely
offsets the Neural Nets.

00:06:51.646 --> 00:06:55.320
So if you're train
on images like these,

00:06:55.321 --> 00:06:56.890
and then you test on
images like these,

00:06:56.891 --> 00:07:00.515
you get really bad errors.

00:07:00.516 --> 00:07:04.020
The error rates go.

00:07:04.021 --> 00:07:09.255
For example, from a say,

00:07:09.256 --> 00:07:13.140
six point five percent to
34 percent or something.

00:07:13.141 --> 00:07:15.440
I'm not going to
explain all of these,

00:07:15.441 --> 00:07:16.925
but basically you've
train on one of

00:07:16.926 --> 00:07:18.820
the data sets that has

00:07:18.821 --> 00:07:20.400
one way or the other of changing

00:07:20.401 --> 00:07:23.300
the spec the fourier
characteristics,

00:07:23.301 --> 00:07:25.215
and then you test on the others

00:07:25.216 --> 00:07:27.870
and humans would
still see the objects,

00:07:27.871 --> 00:07:30.065
which are the thing that
matter very clearly,

00:07:30.066 --> 00:07:33.280
but those net networks
would get fulled.

00:07:33.281 --> 00:07:35.310
You can of course, considerably

00:07:35.311 --> 00:07:36.950
reduce those effects by training

00:07:36.951 --> 00:07:41.160
the network on all of
these types of data,

00:07:41.161 --> 00:07:44.260
just like you can reduce
the effect of versatile exam

00:07:44.261 --> 00:07:46.520
of a show examples

00:07:46.521 --> 00:07:48.085
by training with
our gestural examples.

00:07:48.086 --> 00:07:50.420
But of course,

00:07:50.421 --> 00:07:53.240
then there would be
something else that shows up,

00:07:53.241 --> 00:07:55.625
because the network
probably still

00:07:55.626 --> 00:07:57.060
didn't really capture the

00:07:57.061 --> 00:07:59.905
object nets that
we have in mind.

00:07:59.906 --> 00:08:02.910
So why is this important?

00:08:02.911 --> 00:08:09.545
As we are deploying Machine
Learning in the real world,

00:08:09.546 --> 00:08:11.965
what happens is that,

00:08:11.966 --> 00:08:15.760
the kind of data on which
those systems will be used,

00:08:15.761 --> 00:08:18.300
is almost for sure are
going to be statistically

00:08:18.301 --> 00:08:22.000
different from the kind of
data on which it was trained.

00:08:22.001 --> 00:08:24.360
And as an example of

00:08:24.361 --> 00:08:28.725
this consider self
driving cars or vehicles,

00:08:28.726 --> 00:08:32.240
for which would like
them to behave well in

00:08:32.241 --> 00:08:35.325
these rare but dangerous states.

00:08:35.326 --> 00:08:39.190
And unfortunately, with
the kind of models we have now,

00:08:39.191 --> 00:08:40.910
train which provides learning,

00:08:40.911 --> 00:08:43.710
if these examples are rare,

00:08:43.711 --> 00:08:45.670
they're probably not going
to be learned very well.

00:08:45.671 --> 00:08:49.470
And [inaudible] to
these special kinds of

00:08:49.471 --> 00:08:51.590
domains like near
accident situations

00:08:51.591 --> 00:08:55.300
like in the picture,
might be difficult.

00:08:55.301 --> 00:08:58.610
So, humans are actually pretty

00:08:58.611 --> 00:09:00.680
good at dealing with such cases.

00:09:00.681 --> 00:09:04.130
I only had one accident
in my life, right.

00:09:04.131 --> 00:09:06.870
And how did they do
before the accident?

00:09:06.871 --> 00:09:08.240
And then how do
they do after that?

00:09:08.241 --> 00:09:12.665
We'd only use
single example link.

00:09:12.666 --> 00:09:16.340
We don't need to die

00:09:16.341 --> 00:09:18.260
a thousand deaths to know how to

00:09:18.261 --> 00:09:20.755
prevent dying while we drive.

00:09:20.756 --> 00:09:25.780
What's the catch here?

00:09:25.781 --> 00:09:28.830
My intuition about
this is very simple.

00:09:28.831 --> 00:09:32.020
We have a mental model

00:09:32.021 --> 00:09:35.560
that captures
the explanatory factors

00:09:35.561 --> 00:09:37.060
of our world to some extent.

00:09:37.061 --> 00:09:38.940
It's not perfect.

00:09:38.941 --> 00:09:41.140
And we can generalize to

00:09:41.141 --> 00:09:43.640
new configurations of
the existing factor.

00:09:43.641 --> 00:09:45.700
We already know about trucks and

00:09:45.701 --> 00:09:48.305
cars and the physics
of objects and driving.

00:09:48.306 --> 00:09:49.880
And we already know about

00:09:49.881 --> 00:09:52.850
social behavior and all kinds
of general knowledge that

00:09:52.851 --> 00:09:55.160
allow us to intuitively

00:09:55.161 --> 00:09:57.770
know quickly what's
the right thing to do,

00:09:57.771 --> 00:09:59.520
and many new situations

00:09:59.521 --> 00:10:02.050
completely different
from those we've seen.

00:10:02.051 --> 00:10:04.840
Because they involve concepts
that we already know,

00:10:04.841 --> 00:10:08.590
they just combine
in very new ways.

00:10:08.591 --> 00:10:11.470
Instead, our current
machine learning would

00:10:11.471 --> 00:10:13.835
just tend to drop dead on

00:10:13.836 --> 00:10:19.630
these statistically very
different situations.

00:10:19.631 --> 00:10:24.580
I think we need to

00:10:24.581 --> 00:10:28.620
make serious progress on

00:10:28.621 --> 00:10:29.790
the ability of our models to

00:10:29.791 --> 00:10:32.780
discover and understand
the underlying explanation,

00:10:32.781 --> 00:10:36.950
the underlying causal
relationships so that they

00:10:36.951 --> 00:10:42.020
can make valid predictions
in scenarios,

00:10:42.021 --> 00:10:43.150
in situations that are very,

00:10:43.151 --> 00:10:46.315
very far from anything
that's been seen.

00:10:46.316 --> 00:10:48.355
And that can be important

00:10:48.356 --> 00:10:50.410
also when you consider
machines at plan.

00:10:50.411 --> 00:10:53.480
Because one of the reasons
we're planning is

00:10:53.481 --> 00:10:57.420
to avoid those situations
like dying in an accident.

00:10:57.421 --> 00:11:00.750
But if we don't have
a good way to project

00:11:00.751 --> 00:11:03.710
ourselves into these
situations that are very,

00:11:03.711 --> 00:11:06.190
very different from
the ones we typically see,

00:11:06.191 --> 00:11:09.970
then we won't be
able to do that.

00:11:09.971 --> 00:11:13.460
As I've been saying for
more than a decade now,

00:11:13.461 --> 00:11:17.355
it's all about abstraction
and deep learning as

00:11:17.356 --> 00:11:22.140
we conceived it
more than a decade ago,

00:11:22.141 --> 00:11:26.880
really was about learning
multiple levels of abstraction.

00:11:26.881 --> 00:11:29.830
And in one of the ideas

00:11:29.831 --> 00:11:33.570
that we proposed pretty
early is it'll be nice

00:11:33.571 --> 00:11:36.340
if we had algorithms that

00:11:36.341 --> 00:11:44.305
can separate out
these explanatory factors.

00:11:44.306 --> 00:11:46.350
The phrase we use is

00:11:46.351 --> 00:11:47.970
disentangle the factors
of variation.

00:11:47.971 --> 00:11:50.330
The last NEBS there was
a workshop dedicated to

00:11:50.331 --> 00:11:52.880
this question and it's

00:11:52.881 --> 00:11:54.985
still not clear
exactly what it means.

00:11:54.986 --> 00:11:56.985
But different people have

00:11:56.986 --> 00:11:59.300
different ways of trying
to formalize this idea,

00:11:59.301 --> 00:12:02.780
and I think we should
continue to try to do that.

00:12:02.781 --> 00:12:10.790
This notion of
disentangling is related,

00:12:10.791 --> 00:12:13.230
but different from
the notion of invariance,

00:12:13.231 --> 00:12:16.670
as being a very important notion
and computer visions,

00:12:16.671 --> 00:12:18.185
speech recognition and so on,

00:12:18.186 --> 00:12:21.090
where we'd like to
build detectors and

00:12:21.091 --> 00:12:23.360
features that are invariant

00:12:23.361 --> 00:12:24.810
to the things we
don't care about.

00:12:24.811 --> 00:12:27.120
But sensitive to the things
we do care about.

00:12:27.121 --> 00:12:31.570
But if we're trying to
explain the world around us,

00:12:31.571 --> 00:12:32.630
if we're trying
to build machines

00:12:32.631 --> 00:12:33.590
that explain the world around

00:12:33.591 --> 00:12:38.020
them that understand
their environment,

00:12:39.270 --> 00:12:43.535
we should be prudent about

00:12:43.536 --> 00:12:48.090
what aspects of the world

00:12:48.091 --> 00:12:50.240
we would like our systems
to be invariant about.

00:12:50.241 --> 00:12:53.615
Maybe we want to
capture everything.

00:12:53.616 --> 00:12:57.370
And the most important aspect

00:12:57.371 --> 00:12:59.010
isn't what we get rid of or not,

00:12:59.011 --> 00:13:00.300
but rather how we can

00:13:00.301 --> 00:13:03.165
separate the different factors
from each other.

00:13:03.166 --> 00:13:07.690
If you're doing unsupervised
learning say with speech,

00:13:07.691 --> 00:13:09.830
you'd like to have features that

00:13:09.831 --> 00:13:12.090
capture the phonemes and

00:13:12.091 --> 00:13:14.520
you'd like to have features
that capture the speaker.

00:13:14.521 --> 00:13:18.410
And even if at the end
of the day you get rid of

00:13:18.411 --> 00:13:23.090
the speaker ID and you only
care about what's being said,

00:13:23.091 --> 00:13:26.865
it works if later
you decide that,

00:13:26.866 --> 00:13:29.220
well, what you care
about is the speaker ID

00:13:29.221 --> 00:13:31.430
then, that also works.

00:13:31.431 --> 00:13:34.480
The other thing that
happens with disentangling

00:13:34.481 --> 00:13:35.850
is it's going to

00:13:35.851 --> 00:13:38.250
help us to deal with
the curse of dimensionality.

00:13:38.251 --> 00:13:40.180
It is a good rule of

00:13:40.181 --> 00:13:44.005
thumb to understand what
a good representation is.

00:13:44.006 --> 00:13:49.170
The idea is when we transform
the data in the space,

00:13:49.171 --> 00:13:51.400
machine learning becomes easier.

00:13:51.401 --> 00:13:53.145
So, in particular,

00:13:53.146 --> 00:13:54.770
the kind of complex

00:13:54.771 --> 00:13:56.160
dependencies that
we see in them, say,

00:13:56.161 --> 00:13:59.400
the pixel space will
become easy to model

00:13:59.401 --> 00:14:00.720
maybe with linear models or

00:14:00.721 --> 00:14:04.020
factorize models in that space.

00:14:04.021 --> 00:14:07.010
And there are
many instances of this.

00:14:07.011 --> 00:14:09.690
One of the early ideas
which has given rise

00:14:09.691 --> 00:14:12.715
to a number of unsupervised
learning methods in

00:14:12.716 --> 00:14:16.635
deep learning is that
we are going to learn

00:14:16.636 --> 00:14:19.875
a two-way map or

00:14:19.876 --> 00:14:23.640
gobalistic transformations
between the data space,

00:14:23.641 --> 00:14:26.740
pixel space or whatever and
some representations space.

00:14:26.741 --> 00:14:28.880
So, we have encoders
and decoders and by now,

00:14:28.881 --> 00:14:30.530
everybody uses those terms.

00:14:30.531 --> 00:14:33.030
And so, what do we want

00:14:33.031 --> 00:14:35.210
from our encoder and what
do we want from the decoder.

00:14:35.211 --> 00:14:38.105
Okay, so one is sort of
the inverse of the other.

00:14:38.106 --> 00:14:40.680
But one of the early insights

00:14:40.681 --> 00:14:46.155
from around 2010 or something,

00:14:46.156 --> 00:14:49.400
is that what the encoder

00:14:49.401 --> 00:14:51.750
does is take
the data distribution,

00:14:51.751 --> 00:14:54.540
which is complicated, here is
represented as a spaghetti.

00:14:54.541 --> 00:14:56.240
So, the set of points
corresponding say to

00:14:56.241 --> 00:14:58.810
images is along this curve.

00:14:58.811 --> 00:15:00.970
So we have this spaghetti
that we, of course,

00:15:00.971 --> 00:15:02.230
we don't know where
the spaghetti is

00:15:02.231 --> 00:15:03.840
but we'd like to
do is to flatten

00:15:03.841 --> 00:15:05.420
the spaghetti into something

00:15:05.421 --> 00:15:07.615
that is going to
be easy to model.

00:15:07.616 --> 00:15:09.200
Once it's flat, then it's

00:15:09.201 --> 00:15:11.580
like maybe a Gaussian
or something.

00:15:11.581 --> 00:15:15.680
And predicting things in
that space become very easy.

00:15:15.681 --> 00:15:17.735
Also, if you're able to do that,

00:15:17.736 --> 00:15:20.105
so you know this
two-way transformation,

00:15:20.106 --> 00:15:23.290
generating becomes
easy too because if

00:15:23.291 --> 00:15:26.630
the manifold of points is
now almost a straight line,

00:15:26.631 --> 00:15:29.070
then generating points in
a straight line is very easy.

00:15:29.071 --> 00:15:31.355
And then, I apply the
inverse mapping, the decoder,

00:15:31.356 --> 00:15:32.760
and I get points in the image

00:15:32.761 --> 00:15:34.405
space that looks like images.

00:15:34.406 --> 00:15:37.810
So, that's sort of
one geometric interpretation.

00:15:37.811 --> 00:15:40.100
It also comes in with this idea

00:15:40.101 --> 00:15:43.105
that in the high dimension,

00:15:43.106 --> 00:15:45.600
we have what's called
Marginal independence.

00:15:45.601 --> 00:15:47.480
In other words, I can

00:15:47.481 --> 00:15:50.880
assemble from each
of the factors if

00:15:50.881 --> 00:15:52.120
dimension is independently they

00:15:52.121 --> 00:15:53.550
all depend on each other so

00:15:53.551 --> 00:15:56.600
it's very easy to model
distribution in that space,

00:15:56.601 --> 00:15:59.805
whereas, modeling directly in
that space is kind of hard.

00:15:59.806 --> 00:16:01.910
So, that's one view
that's kind of

00:16:01.911 --> 00:16:04.265
interesting that
came up pretty early.

00:16:04.266 --> 00:16:07.595
And some of the algorithms
tried to do that explicitly.

00:16:07.596 --> 00:16:10.280
One of the things we
did also pretty early

00:16:10.281 --> 00:16:18.150
is think about how that
affects sampling methods.

00:16:18.151 --> 00:16:20.510
But here, because something

00:16:20.511 --> 00:16:22.990
on the flattened space
is easy but here,

00:16:22.991 --> 00:16:25.970
I'm showing
sort of toy experiment

00:16:25.971 --> 00:16:27.505
to illustrate what happens

00:16:27.506 --> 00:16:29.950
when you do this flattening

00:16:29.951 --> 00:16:32.470
with a very simple algorithm
for representation learning,

00:16:32.471 --> 00:16:35.240
which from those days these

00:16:35.241 --> 00:16:38.300
were stacks of
denoising autoencoders.

00:16:38.301 --> 00:16:42.740
So, what we're going
to be doing is again,

00:16:42.741 --> 00:16:43.930
we going take
the spaghetti here,

00:16:43.931 --> 00:16:47.530
I just represented it
as a curved manifold.

00:16:47.531 --> 00:16:50.310
And it's going to
be transformed into

00:16:50.311 --> 00:16:53.740
the hidden unit space
into something flat.

00:16:53.741 --> 00:16:55.470
And so, one interesting question

00:16:55.471 --> 00:16:56.610
is how do we know
that it's flat?

00:16:56.611 --> 00:16:58.470
So, here's a very
simple way that

00:16:58.471 --> 00:17:00.460
you can check that
a manifold of points is flat.

00:17:00.461 --> 00:17:02.450
You take two points
on the manifold.

00:17:02.451 --> 00:17:06.845
So, two images and you
take their average.

00:17:06.846 --> 00:17:08.465
So, you interpolate
between them.

00:17:08.466 --> 00:17:13.270
And if the thing in between
looks like an image,

00:17:13.271 --> 00:17:16.415
then it means that
the manifold is flat.

00:17:16.416 --> 00:17:19.750
So, if you take
these two images and I

00:17:19.751 --> 00:17:21.360
take the average
and the thing in

00:17:21.361 --> 00:17:23.600
between is also on the manifold,

00:17:23.601 --> 00:17:24.990
then the manifold is flat.

00:17:24.991 --> 00:17:27.325
If it's true for
every linear combination,

00:17:27.326 --> 00:17:31.455
then you know, everything
sits on that convex set.

00:17:31.456 --> 00:17:33.630
But if the manifold is curved,

00:17:33.631 --> 00:17:36.650
then the things in between
will not be on the manifold.

00:17:36.651 --> 00:17:38.305
They will not look like images.

00:17:38.306 --> 00:17:39.840
So, we can do that experiment.

00:17:39.841 --> 00:17:41.470
So, we can take
like this image of

00:17:41.471 --> 00:17:43.850
a nine and image of a three.

00:17:43.851 --> 00:17:46.670
And if we work in pixel space

00:17:46.671 --> 00:17:49.230
and we just do averages
linear combinations,

00:17:49.231 --> 00:17:51.580
we get these images
which obviously,

00:17:51.581 --> 00:17:53.430
are just pasting
a nine and three

00:17:53.431 --> 00:17:56.520
together but don't look
like natural digits.

00:17:56.521 --> 00:17:59.750
However, if we take the
same nine and we project

00:17:59.751 --> 00:18:01.910
it in the representation space

00:18:01.911 --> 00:18:03.840
of some unsupervised learner.

00:18:03.841 --> 00:18:07.920
And then, we do the averaging
in that H-space.

00:18:07.921 --> 00:18:11.690
And then, we take
these linear combinations

00:18:11.691 --> 00:18:13.795
and we project them
back using the decoder.

00:18:13.796 --> 00:18:15.740
We can look at
the corresponding images.

00:18:15.741 --> 00:18:17.345
And what we see is that

00:18:17.346 --> 00:18:21.015
the corresponding images
look like natural digits,

00:18:21.016 --> 00:18:23.520
all the way along
the interpolation line.

00:18:23.521 --> 00:18:24.950
And the two lines
here correspond

00:18:24.951 --> 00:18:26.100
to interpolating a two-layer,

00:18:26.101 --> 00:18:27.920
as the first layer
and the second layer.

00:18:27.921 --> 00:18:31.140
And there's more than that,

00:18:31.141 --> 00:18:32.420
which is that if as we

00:18:32.421 --> 00:18:35.770
move interpolating from
this image to this image.

00:18:35.771 --> 00:18:39.150
So now, I imagine
this curved manifold,

00:18:39.151 --> 00:18:42.320
just for illustration
just like in the picture.

00:18:42.321 --> 00:18:45.340
What we see is that
it's going to be nine,

00:18:45.341 --> 00:18:46.760
nine, nine, nine,
nine, nine, nine,

00:18:46.761 --> 00:18:47.790
nine and suddenly there's like

00:18:47.791 --> 00:18:50.015
a very quick transition
where it becomes a three.

00:18:50.016 --> 00:18:52.975
And it's all threes
up to this three.

00:18:52.976 --> 00:18:55.380
And the junction
that is something

00:18:55.381 --> 00:18:58.180
that's in between
a nine and a three,

00:18:58.181 --> 00:19:02.085
but still preserves either
one's or the other's identity.

00:19:02.086 --> 00:19:03.530
So, all of this
gives us some nice

00:19:03.531 --> 00:19:05.230
intuitions about the geometry

00:19:05.231 --> 00:19:08.210
behind what we'd like

00:19:08.211 --> 00:19:11.160
to have in these
higher level spaces.

00:19:11.161 --> 00:19:14.235
And so, there's been
a lot of work in applying

00:19:14.236 --> 00:19:18.455
these ideas to
generative models.

00:19:18.456 --> 00:19:23.460
As Susan mentioned, one of
the really hot family of

00:19:23.461 --> 00:19:25.840
algorithms are these GANs

00:19:25.841 --> 00:19:29.770
that we started off
three years ago.

00:19:29.771 --> 00:19:32.730
And they are pretty amazing,

00:19:32.731 --> 00:19:33.865
I'm not going to describe them.

00:19:33.866 --> 00:19:35.785
But I think we are still far off

00:19:35.786 --> 00:19:39.680
the mark. So, what's missing?

00:19:39.681 --> 00:19:42.340
As I've been saying, if you

00:19:42.341 --> 00:19:45.460
analyze the errors
made by those systems,

00:19:45.461 --> 00:19:47.725
both for recognition
and for generation,

00:19:47.726 --> 00:19:50.695
you can see that
they clearly don't

00:19:50.696 --> 00:19:54.250
understand the world
in the way that we do.

00:19:54.251 --> 00:19:55.770
And they are missing

00:19:55.771 --> 00:19:59.855
often the point,
the crucial abstractions.

00:19:59.856 --> 00:20:04.935
So, what's needed?
More abstract representations.

00:20:04.936 --> 00:20:09.985
One aspect of this I think

00:20:09.986 --> 00:20:18.340
is that our learning theories
also have to be modified.

00:20:18.341 --> 00:20:20.410
Current machine
learning theories

00:20:20.411 --> 00:20:22.440
anchored in the IID assumption,

00:20:22.441 --> 00:20:23.960
assuming that
the task distribution is

00:20:23.961 --> 00:20:26.600
going to be the same as
a training distribution.

00:20:26.601 --> 00:20:32.430
And so we can get very
confident about our models,

00:20:32.431 --> 00:20:34.230
but when we go out of

00:20:34.231 --> 00:20:36.670
that assumption,
things can break down.

00:20:36.671 --> 00:20:40.495
Somehow, humans
are able to do well

00:20:40.496 --> 00:20:45.935
as I said earlier,
so what's missing?

00:20:45.936 --> 00:20:49.712
So there's this idea of
learning Disentangled

00:20:49.713 --> 00:20:53.320
Representations, and
one thing I tried to do in

00:20:53.321 --> 00:20:57.355
a 2013 review paper on
representational learning

00:20:57.356 --> 00:21:06.160
is present the idea that
we're not going to get very,

00:21:06.161 --> 00:21:07.540
very good machine learning

00:21:07.541 --> 00:21:09.920
without introducing some priors,

00:21:09.921 --> 00:21:11.640
some assumptions
about the world.

00:21:11.641 --> 00:21:14.140
Deep learning is starting with

00:21:14.141 --> 00:21:17.070
an assumption that
this composition of

00:21:17.071 --> 00:21:20.940
different layers is appropriate

00:21:20.941 --> 00:21:22.330
for the data we want to model.

00:21:22.331 --> 00:21:24.860
That there is some
compositionality that can be

00:21:24.861 --> 00:21:27.860
captured with these
families of functions.

00:21:27.861 --> 00:21:31.560
But there are many other
assumptions that are as broad,

00:21:31.561 --> 00:21:36.520
as generic that some
of our models use,

00:21:36.521 --> 00:21:41.780
and I think we should continue
adding more elements to

00:21:41.781 --> 00:21:44.620
this list of priorities that

00:21:44.621 --> 00:21:45.910
we put in our models in order

00:21:45.911 --> 00:21:47.845
to improve a generalization,

00:21:47.846 --> 00:21:50.410
but ideally try to do it in
a way that remains fairly

00:21:50.411 --> 00:21:53.770
general and not
too task specific.

00:21:53.771 --> 00:21:55.410
So, these include things

00:21:55.411 --> 00:22:00.450
like exploiting the fact that
there are different scales,

00:22:00.451 --> 00:22:02.210
both spatially and temporally,

00:22:02.211 --> 00:22:03.690
and we already have
models that do that,

00:22:03.691 --> 00:22:05.740
but I think there's a lot
more to exploit there.

00:22:05.741 --> 00:22:07.200
I already mentioned

00:22:07.201 --> 00:22:09.375
Marginal Independence, in
other words the idea that,

00:22:09.376 --> 00:22:11.210
if you take the data and you

00:22:11.211 --> 00:22:13.110
transform it into
the right space,

00:22:13.111 --> 00:22:16.515
then all of the factors
become independent.

00:22:16.516 --> 00:22:20.250
Something I already mentioned
earlier is this idea that,

00:22:20.251 --> 00:22:24.905
when we move the data
in the abstract space,

00:22:24.906 --> 00:22:27.590
the dependencies
between the factors,

00:22:27.591 --> 00:22:29.845
the variables in that space
become really simple.

00:22:29.846 --> 00:22:32.520
And later I'll talk to you
about the consciousness prior

00:22:32.521 --> 00:22:36.085
which is one way
to implement this.

00:22:36.086 --> 00:22:43.270
And then, another idea I
mentioned is that of causality.

00:22:43.271 --> 00:22:46.150
So, I think the whole area

00:22:46.151 --> 00:22:49.125
of causal machine learning
is still very

00:22:49.126 --> 00:22:51.480
young and I wish

00:22:51.481 --> 00:22:53.160
there would be
more people exploring this

00:22:53.161 --> 00:22:55.110
because I believe this

00:22:55.111 --> 00:22:56.910
is something that humans
take advantage of,

00:22:56.911 --> 00:22:58.880
and it's one of the ingredients
that allows us to

00:22:58.881 --> 00:23:02.190
generalize to these very
different scenarios.

00:23:02.191 --> 00:23:05.335
And I'll mention
some work we started,

00:23:05.336 --> 00:23:10.265
what I call controllable factors
in that direction.

00:23:10.266 --> 00:23:14.520
So if we look

00:23:14.521 --> 00:23:17.870
at how humans learn in
a very autonomous way,

00:23:17.871 --> 00:23:19.360
like look at all the knowledge

00:23:19.361 --> 00:23:21.135
that babies have
by two year old,

00:23:21.136 --> 00:23:25.680
like intuitive
physics, one thing

00:23:25.681 --> 00:23:29.000
that is interesting to consider

00:23:29.001 --> 00:23:30.300
is how much they interact with

00:23:30.301 --> 00:23:33.480
their environment in order
to acquire that knowledge.

00:23:33.481 --> 00:23:37.870
And I now think that

00:23:37.871 --> 00:23:44.440
for upcoming progress towards
human level intelligence,

00:23:44.441 --> 00:23:47.990
machine learning needs to
move more towards this notion

00:23:47.991 --> 00:23:51.270
that acting in the world

00:23:51.271 --> 00:23:55.880
to acquire information is a
very, very important tool.

00:23:55.881 --> 00:23:59.985
Humans do it and I think this

00:23:59.986 --> 00:24:03.930
is something that we need
to pay more attention to.

00:24:03.931 --> 00:24:05.530
And of course, the basic tools

00:24:05.531 --> 00:24:07.240
from that come from
reinforcement learning,

00:24:07.241 --> 00:24:09.520
but we may have to
reinvent a bit of

00:24:09.521 --> 00:24:14.020
reinforcement learning to move
further in that direction.

00:24:14.021 --> 00:24:16.840
So, one particular aspect that

00:24:16.841 --> 00:24:20.480
we explored is this idea
of controllability.

00:24:20.481 --> 00:24:23.270
I'm looking for a prop here.

00:24:23.271 --> 00:24:30.095
Let's see. So, here's a prop.

00:24:30.096 --> 00:24:34.745
I just made up a policy

00:24:34.746 --> 00:24:38.090
here to control
this sheet of paper.

00:24:38.091 --> 00:24:40.815
I can move it around in
space, I can fold it.

00:24:40.816 --> 00:24:42.280
I can do all kinds
of fun things with

00:24:42.281 --> 00:24:44.250
it and I just made it up.

00:24:44.251 --> 00:24:47.545
I've made up these policies

00:24:47.546 --> 00:24:52.180
to control different aspects
of this object.

00:24:52.181 --> 00:24:58.225
So, what this
illustrates is that,

00:24:58.226 --> 00:25:02.490
our brain can come up
with control policies

00:25:02.491 --> 00:25:05.910
that influence specific
aspects of the world,

00:25:05.911 --> 00:25:08.430
like the position of
this object and different,

00:25:08.431 --> 00:25:11.680
complicated geometrical
attributes here about its folding.

00:25:11.681 --> 00:25:16.710
And clearly my brain can
represent that information so,

00:25:16.711 --> 00:25:19.210
I could report it to you,
I can tell you where it is,

00:25:19.211 --> 00:25:21.125
I can have a mental model of it,

00:25:21.126 --> 00:25:23.125
I can plan based on it.

00:25:23.126 --> 00:25:26.770
And so, we have these two things
happening in parallel.

00:25:26.771 --> 00:25:28.350
We have the representation and

00:25:28.351 --> 00:25:31.440
the policies and they're
matched in some way.

00:25:31.441 --> 00:25:36.140
So, I have policies to affect
one aspect of the world.

00:25:36.141 --> 00:25:37.680
I can decide to move

00:25:37.681 --> 00:25:40.275
just this thing and

00:25:40.276 --> 00:25:42.560
I can represent
that thing in my mind.

00:25:42.561 --> 00:25:46.205
So I'll tell you
more about this.

00:25:46.206 --> 00:25:48.920
But, clearly, in order to

00:25:48.921 --> 00:25:50.490
discover these aspects of

00:25:50.491 --> 00:25:52.225
the world that are controllable,

00:25:52.226 --> 00:25:53.480
which are not all the aspects

00:25:53.481 --> 00:25:54.440
of the world but I
think they give us

00:25:54.441 --> 00:25:55.940
very strong clues
about how the world

00:25:55.941 --> 00:25:57.900
works, you need to be acting.

00:25:57.901 --> 00:26:00.410
You need to be
trying things out.

00:26:00.411 --> 00:26:03.900
And I think as you do that,

00:26:03.901 --> 00:26:06.970
emerges almost naturally for

00:26:06.971 --> 00:26:10.040
the learner
very important notions

00:26:10.041 --> 00:26:11.070
from cognitive science,

00:26:11.071 --> 00:26:13.285
like the notion of
objects and agents.

00:26:13.286 --> 00:26:14.970
Well, the notion
of object emerges

00:26:14.971 --> 00:26:17.305
because the objects are

00:26:17.306 --> 00:26:20.720
the sort of aggregates

00:26:20.721 --> 00:26:22.580
of attributes that I'm
controlling together.

00:26:22.581 --> 00:26:26.280
I controlling x, y and z
and other attributes of

00:26:26.281 --> 00:26:29.520
this thing and they

00:26:29.521 --> 00:26:32.905
are all sort of spatially
coherent as well.

00:26:32.906 --> 00:26:35.760
And that sort of
things that can be

00:26:35.761 --> 00:26:38.190
controlled are
attributes of objects.

00:26:38.191 --> 00:26:42.590
And agents are the entities
that can do that.

00:26:42.591 --> 00:26:47.080
I'm an agent but I can
also see you do some things

00:26:47.081 --> 00:26:49.500
and imagine how I would

00:26:49.501 --> 00:26:52.170
be doing it if I were you,
or something like this.

00:26:52.171 --> 00:26:53.470
And we do that all the time.

00:26:53.471 --> 00:26:55.620
So, the notion of
agents is very,

00:26:55.621 --> 00:26:59.350
very convenient to
model the world once

00:26:59.351 --> 00:27:02.215
we incorporate of course actions

00:27:02.216 --> 00:27:04.330
and trying to control
things in the world.

00:27:04.331 --> 00:27:07.035
Now, there's
something particular

00:27:07.036 --> 00:27:09.320
which sort of blew
my mind a little bit at

00:27:09.321 --> 00:27:11.810
the beginning about
the kind of knowledge

00:27:11.811 --> 00:27:14.750
that an agent acquires by
interacting in the world,

00:27:14.751 --> 00:27:15.995
which is that it's not

00:27:15.996 --> 00:27:19.540
universal knowledge, it's
subjective knowledge.

00:27:19.541 --> 00:27:25.990
So, the policy I have
depends on my body,

00:27:25.991 --> 00:27:30.840
and that's related to what
people call affordances.

00:27:30.841 --> 00:27:32.830
There are things that I can do

00:27:32.831 --> 00:27:35.205
that maybe a baby can't do.

00:27:35.206 --> 00:27:37.410
And so we have a different
vision of the world,

00:27:37.411 --> 00:27:39.935
a different understanding
of what can be done.

00:27:39.936 --> 00:27:43.400
And so, this is different from

00:27:43.401 --> 00:27:47.990
the maybe simple minded view
of AI where there is sort of

00:27:47.991 --> 00:27:51.870
a universal truth
that all of us see and

00:27:51.871 --> 00:27:53.375
all of us can control

00:27:53.376 --> 00:27:56.720
our bodies and what
we are able to do,

00:27:56.721 --> 00:27:59.310
kind of condition
our understanding of the world.

00:27:59.311 --> 00:28:00.925
And it's kind of unfortunate

00:28:00.926 --> 00:28:03.810
but we have to deal with that.

00:28:03.811 --> 00:28:09.590
So, last year, we
started this project of

00:28:09.591 --> 00:28:11.745
trying to put in

00:28:11.746 --> 00:28:15.210
equations and algorithms
and experiments,

00:28:15.211 --> 00:28:19.505
this idea of
controllable factors.

00:28:19.506 --> 00:28:26.580
The idea that one way to
discover good representations,

00:28:26.581 --> 00:28:29.935
or at least some of the factors
in a good representation,

00:28:29.936 --> 00:28:31.640
is that we have clues

00:28:31.641 --> 00:28:34.840
about the existence
of these factors by

00:28:34.841 --> 00:28:36.760
the fact that we can control

00:28:36.761 --> 00:28:38.370
those factors without changing

00:28:38.371 --> 00:28:39.680
too many other things
in the world.

00:28:39.681 --> 00:28:45.250
So, we designed a term

00:28:45.251 --> 00:28:48.485
that would be added in a
training objective of a learner,

00:28:48.486 --> 00:28:51.210
in which we say, "Let's look for

00:28:51.211 --> 00:28:54.340
a policy that is

00:28:54.341 --> 00:28:56.790
going to choose some actions
given some state,

00:28:56.791 --> 00:29:02.050
such that after we apply
the policy, the state changes.

00:29:02.051 --> 00:29:04.945
And, some feature,

00:29:04.946 --> 00:29:08.360
K of the world changes
as much as possible."

00:29:08.361 --> 00:29:12.550
So, the Kth policy is going
to control the Kth factor,

00:29:12.551 --> 00:29:14.640
in the simplest way
to think about this.

00:29:14.641 --> 00:29:17.005
So I'm going to have
policy number K,

00:29:17.006 --> 00:29:19.670
control unit number K in

00:29:19.671 --> 00:29:23.120
my network and you
need to change as

00:29:23.121 --> 00:29:29.145
much as possible while the
other units are not changing,

00:29:29.146 --> 00:29:30.960
or changing as
little as possible.

00:29:30.961 --> 00:29:34.150
So, that's the
starting point of this.

00:29:34.151 --> 00:29:37.000
And we can add that criterion

00:29:37.001 --> 00:29:39.090
to whatever other criteria here,

00:29:39.091 --> 00:29:42.145
it's a simple
reconstruction error.

00:29:42.146 --> 00:29:48.220
So, we've been able to make
this kind of idea work on

00:29:48.221 --> 00:29:50.355
very simple worlds for now and

00:29:50.356 --> 00:29:53.000
still facing some
optimization difficulties

00:29:53.001 --> 00:29:54.010
we don't completely understand.

00:29:54.011 --> 00:29:57.310
But that in little toy
problems like this,

00:29:57.311 --> 00:29:59.200
we learn both an encoder and

00:29:59.201 --> 00:30:01.960
decoder that maps
from pixel space.

00:30:01.961 --> 00:30:05.640
So this is like when we
image space into here,

00:30:05.641 --> 00:30:10.650
a very simple 2D space
corresponding to, I mean,

00:30:10.651 --> 00:30:12.510
it discovers that
what matters in

00:30:12.511 --> 00:30:15.090
this world is
the position of the ball,

00:30:15.091 --> 00:30:19.030
and the reason is
that the only actions

00:30:19.031 --> 00:30:21.675
that the agent can do here
is move the ball around.

00:30:21.676 --> 00:30:22.990
And so, it learns that what

00:30:22.991 --> 00:30:24.310
matters is the position
of the ball.

00:30:24.311 --> 00:30:25.910
And so, the encoder essentially

00:30:25.911 --> 00:30:27.800
just learn to take
images and spit

00:30:27.801 --> 00:30:28.960
out the position of the ball in

00:30:28.961 --> 00:30:32.425
some funny space and
the decoder can mount back.

00:30:32.426 --> 00:30:38.470
So if I play with the
coordinates here and then decode,

00:30:38.471 --> 00:30:41.045
I get a new image where the ball
is in a different place.

00:30:41.046 --> 00:30:43.160
And it turns out
we can interpret

00:30:43.161 --> 00:30:46.225
the changes here directly
in terms of position,

00:30:46.226 --> 00:30:49.270
and it has also separated

00:30:49.271 --> 00:30:50.350
the changes happening in

00:30:50.351 --> 00:30:52.940
one direction versus
the other direction.

00:30:52.941 --> 00:30:56.240
So that's kind of nice.

00:30:56.241 --> 00:30:59.270
You can also do things like take

00:30:59.271 --> 00:31:01.900
two images in that world,

00:31:01.901 --> 00:31:03.715
of course, encode them,

00:31:03.716 --> 00:31:04.940
take the difference
between their

00:31:04.941 --> 00:31:06.130
representations and that will

00:31:06.131 --> 00:31:09.370
tell you basically what

00:31:09.371 --> 00:31:11.390
actions are needed in
order to go from one to

00:31:11.391 --> 00:31:13.540
the other because they

00:31:13.541 --> 00:31:16.830
correspond to how much of
each of the factors you

00:31:16.831 --> 00:31:23.895
should apply in order to
go from one to the other.

00:31:23.896 --> 00:31:28.875
So that was like one-step
actions, one-step policies.

00:31:28.876 --> 00:31:32.140
We've been extending this
to multi-step policies

00:31:32.141 --> 00:31:35.735
and also generalizing from

00:31:35.736 --> 00:31:40.280
a fixed any variable set

00:31:40.281 --> 00:31:42.630
of factors to a common
natural set of factors.

00:31:42.631 --> 00:31:45.850
Because if the kinds

00:31:45.851 --> 00:31:47.040
of factors I'm talking
about are things

00:31:47.041 --> 00:31:48.210
like positions of objects,

00:31:48.211 --> 00:31:50.370
the problem is that
how many objects are there here?

00:31:50.371 --> 00:31:52.035
How many objects are
there in the world?

00:31:52.036 --> 00:31:53.755
Do I need a different neuron for

00:31:53.756 --> 00:31:55.060
each possible object
that you could

00:31:55.061 --> 00:31:56.775
ever see? That doesn't
make sense, right?

00:31:56.776 --> 00:32:00.260
So instead of enumerating
all the factors,

00:32:00.261 --> 00:32:04.770
what you'd like to do is
to have a name for factors.

00:32:04.771 --> 00:32:06.430
And the names is going
to becoming natural,

00:32:06.431 --> 00:32:08.850
like is going to be a vector,

00:32:08.851 --> 00:32:14.120
an embedding. And so, yeah.

00:32:14.121 --> 00:32:18.570
So, we're playing with
these kinds of ideas right now,

00:32:18.571 --> 00:32:22.480
and playing the
same kinds of games.

00:32:22.481 --> 00:32:29.525
But now, the names are vectors,

00:32:29.526 --> 00:32:32.400
and what we're showing here are

00:32:32.401 --> 00:32:34.590
the embeddings of the names

00:32:34.591 --> 00:32:36.340
of factors that
the system discovers.

00:32:36.341 --> 00:32:38.410
And here that it's
the same kind of game as before,

00:32:38.411 --> 00:32:40.375
but now there's just
been more positions

00:32:40.376 --> 00:32:43.135
and it discovers that

00:32:43.136 --> 00:32:45.450
the important factors are

00:32:45.451 --> 00:32:47.000
different positions
corresponding to

00:32:47.001 --> 00:32:48.960
a grid space because

00:32:48.961 --> 00:32:52.465
the world here happens
to have a grid structure.

00:32:52.466 --> 00:32:56.110
Anyways Okay.

00:32:56.111 --> 00:33:00.980
Yeah, I talked about this
already and time is flying.

00:33:00.981 --> 00:33:11.480
So, let me move on to a second
type of exploration which

00:33:11.481 --> 00:33:13.990
touches on I think

00:33:13.991 --> 00:33:16.160
something I'd like to

00:33:16.161 --> 00:33:18.760
revisit in our
training objectives for

00:33:18.761 --> 00:33:23.820
improvised learning and it's

00:33:23.821 --> 00:33:25.590
that they are all in

00:33:25.591 --> 00:33:29.145
pixel space rather than
something like an abstract space.

00:33:29.146 --> 00:33:31.185
So if you look, of
course at likelihood,

00:33:31.186 --> 00:33:34.335
if you look at any kind
of reconstruction error,

00:33:34.336 --> 00:33:38.080
if you look at even things
like gown training objectives,

00:33:38.081 --> 00:33:40.450
they're all focused on what

00:33:40.451 --> 00:33:42.960
is happening in what
I call pixel space,

00:33:42.961 --> 00:33:46.545
but that would be like
acoustic space, or anything,

00:33:46.546 --> 00:33:49.750
video space, cactus space,

00:33:49.751 --> 00:33:51.820
whatever the data space.

00:33:51.821 --> 00:33:55.530
So why is that a problem?

00:33:57.010 --> 00:34:00.840
If we were able
to map the data to

00:34:00.841 --> 00:34:02.800
this better representation as

00:34:02.801 --> 00:34:04.080
I've been talking about then,

00:34:04.081 --> 00:34:06.730
as I said, modeling
in that space,

00:34:06.731 --> 00:34:07.860
planning in that space,

00:34:07.861 --> 00:34:11.430
reasoning in that space would
be so much more convenient.

00:34:11.431 --> 00:34:14.305
But actually, it's
a chicken-and-egg thing.

00:34:14.306 --> 00:34:18.600
We're not going to

00:34:18.601 --> 00:34:21.310
be given like magically
those representations,

00:34:21.311 --> 00:34:23.515
so we'd like to have an
objective function that really

00:34:23.516 --> 00:34:27.195
focuses on the abstract space.

00:34:27.196 --> 00:34:29.690
And to see a little bit what

00:34:29.691 --> 00:34:32.405
can go wrong with
our current methods,

00:34:32.406 --> 00:34:36.840
let me share a little bit
of a couple of years of

00:34:36.841 --> 00:34:40.660
experience with trying to

00:34:40.661 --> 00:34:43.300
do speech synthesis
with Neural Nets.

00:34:43.301 --> 00:34:47.240
We made a lot of progress
now to the point where

00:34:47.241 --> 00:34:49.020
now Google is putting

00:34:49.021 --> 00:34:52.895
those kinds of Neural Nets
into their speech synthesizer.

00:34:52.896 --> 00:34:58.670
But, when we started
this research,

00:34:58.671 --> 00:35:01.860
we were very
ambitious and we said,

00:35:01.861 --> 00:35:03.290
"Okay, let's do like a pure,

00:35:03.291 --> 00:35:05.305
hardcore improvised learning,

00:35:05.306 --> 00:35:07.665
take hundred hours of speech,

00:35:07.666 --> 00:35:14.615
and train a huge complicated
recurrent network to model

00:35:14.616 --> 00:35:20.000
what speech is sequences of

00:35:20.001 --> 00:35:25.945
16 thousand samples per
second for multiple seconds."

00:35:25.946 --> 00:35:30.090
And what happens with
our current best models,

00:35:30.091 --> 00:35:31.890
if they're trained in
a purely improvised way.

00:35:31.891 --> 00:35:33.410
In other words, you
don't give them words and

00:35:33.411 --> 00:35:36.575
phonemes is that they produce

00:35:36.576 --> 00:35:40.130
speech that sound like someone

00:35:40.131 --> 00:35:41.680
speaking in other
European language

00:35:41.681 --> 00:35:43.305
if he's train on English.

00:35:43.306 --> 00:35:46.890
But it's something
like there's no words,

00:35:46.891 --> 00:35:49.290
this is like gibberish.

00:35:49.291 --> 00:35:51.770
So these models capture

00:35:51.771 --> 00:35:55.420
the texture of speech to
capture how speech sounds like,

00:35:55.421 --> 00:35:57.200
but they failed completely to

00:35:57.201 --> 00:35:58.700
capture the longer term

00:35:58.701 --> 00:36:00.955
structure, the
linguistic structure.

00:36:00.956 --> 00:36:03.160
Now, there's
a very easy fix to this,

00:36:03.161 --> 00:36:05.090
and this is what you have in
a speech synthesis systems

00:36:05.091 --> 00:36:07.435
and all of the systems
that people use these days,

00:36:07.436 --> 00:36:11.705
which is used separately
train the model that,

00:36:11.706 --> 00:36:14.630
is you use improvised learning.

00:36:14.631 --> 00:36:16.160
You separately train
a model that goes

00:36:16.161 --> 00:36:18.070
from phonemes to acoustics.

00:36:18.071 --> 00:36:20.890
And from a model that just

00:36:20.891 --> 00:36:22.950
captures the statistics of

00:36:22.951 --> 00:36:24.040
sequences of
phonemes or in other

00:36:24.041 --> 00:36:26.245
words, a language model.

00:36:26.246 --> 00:36:29.105
And that works perfectly well.

00:36:29.106 --> 00:36:32.040
Now, we can generate
unconditionally by

00:36:32.041 --> 00:36:33.400
just first sampling a sequence

00:36:33.401 --> 00:36:34.930
of words or a sequence
of phonemes,

00:36:34.931 --> 00:36:39.480
and then conditioning
on that generate sounds.

00:36:39.481 --> 00:36:42.580
So, besides the fact that we

00:36:42.581 --> 00:36:45.740
have a fix by using
that knowledge,

00:36:45.741 --> 00:36:48.610
I think what this
teaches us is that

00:36:48.611 --> 00:36:51.540
our improvised learning
mechanisms have not been able to

00:36:51.541 --> 00:36:54.670
discover something that
should be incredibly salient,

00:36:54.671 --> 00:36:56.130
which is the presence
of phonemes

00:36:56.131 --> 00:36:57.650
as the characteristics
of speech.

00:36:57.651 --> 00:37:00.570
If you do like KEY means
clustering on acoustic signals,

00:37:00.571 --> 00:37:02.530
you discover immediately
phonemes, right?

00:37:02.531 --> 00:37:04.160
Or, at least broadly speaking,

00:37:04.161 --> 00:37:06.295
it's very salient
statistical structure.

00:37:06.296 --> 00:37:09.370
How is it that these models
haven't been able to discover

00:37:09.371 --> 00:37:12.350
them and then see

00:37:12.351 --> 00:37:16.440
that there's like this really
powerful part of the signal

00:37:16.441 --> 00:37:24.095
which is explained by the
dependencies between phonemes?

00:37:24.096 --> 00:37:25.855
And the reason is, I think,

00:37:25.856 --> 00:37:28.880
simply that that part
of the signal occupies

00:37:28.881 --> 00:37:30.380
very few bits in
the total number

00:37:30.381 --> 00:37:31.840
of bits that isn't
the signal, right?

00:37:31.841 --> 00:37:33.910
So, the rows in this signal is

00:37:33.911 --> 00:37:36.965
16 thousand real
numbers per second.

00:37:36.966 --> 00:37:42.135
How many phonemes per
second do you get?

00:37:42.136 --> 00:37:45.205
Well, I don't know, 10,

00:37:45.206 --> 00:37:48.470
right? or maybe 16.

00:37:48.471 --> 00:37:51.195
So there's a factor of
a thousand in terms of

00:37:51.196 --> 00:37:53.290
how many bits of information are

00:37:53.291 --> 00:37:55.670
carried by the word level,

00:37:55.671 --> 00:37:57.290
phoneme level information versus

00:37:57.291 --> 00:37:58.915
the acoustic level information.

00:37:58.916 --> 00:38:00.650
And the long length queue,

00:38:00.651 --> 00:38:02.150
or any other criteria we use,

00:38:02.151 --> 00:38:07.405
as I said earlier, they're
focusing on these details.

00:38:07.406 --> 00:38:11.440
And so, yeah. As our models

00:38:11.441 --> 00:38:13.040
get better and
better every year,

00:38:13.041 --> 00:38:15.640
the approach, something at

00:38:15.641 --> 00:38:18.175
the higher level, but
it's very painful.

00:38:18.176 --> 00:38:22.880
Maybe we need to change
how we train these things.

00:38:22.881 --> 00:38:28.145
So now, let me tell you about

00:38:28.146 --> 00:38:30.490
a direction of
research that we've

00:38:30.491 --> 00:38:35.275
started which
attempts to fix this,

00:38:35.276 --> 00:38:41.495
and it's very much inspired
by cognitive psychology,

00:38:41.496 --> 00:38:44.290
and very old work

00:38:44.291 --> 00:38:51.050
actually about attention
and consciousness.

00:38:51.051 --> 00:38:55.475
So one part of the idea is,

00:38:55.476 --> 00:38:58.150
we want to design
objective functions,

00:38:58.151 --> 00:39:02.630
where say, we could
have encoders,

00:39:02.631 --> 00:39:03.680
but we don't need decoders.

00:39:03.681 --> 00:39:04.830
Where the objective function

00:39:04.831 --> 00:39:05.970
is going to be defined purely in

00:39:05.971 --> 00:39:09.270
the abstract space.
That's part of it.

00:39:09.271 --> 00:39:11.000
But there's something
else here which is,

00:39:11.001 --> 00:39:14.740
I think fairly new and
connects with classical work

00:39:14.741 --> 00:39:19.660
in the eye and knowledge
representation and symbolically.

00:39:19.661 --> 00:39:23.325
So, think about your thoughts,

00:39:23.326 --> 00:39:26.695
think about your conscious
thoughts more precisely.

00:39:26.696 --> 00:39:30.540
What happens is, at
any particular moment,

00:39:30.541 --> 00:39:33.095
there is something
that comes to your mind

00:39:33.096 --> 00:39:39.005
and it's very low
dimensional, that's my claim.

00:39:39.006 --> 00:39:45.715
You can convert that
into sounds, a sentence.

00:39:45.716 --> 00:39:47.950
Not everything is like this.

00:39:47.951 --> 00:39:50.540
For example, I can do visual
imagery and that's hard to

00:39:50.541 --> 00:39:54.220
verbalize but many other things
I can verbalize.

00:39:54.221 --> 00:39:56.235
But even if it's visual,

00:39:56.236 --> 00:39:57.600
it's very low dimensional.

00:39:57.601 --> 00:40:00.145
It concerns very few
aspects of the world.

00:40:00.146 --> 00:40:04.760
So, why do we have
this thing which seems

00:40:04.761 --> 00:40:08.985
to focus on so few aspects of
reality at a time it seems,

00:40:08.986 --> 00:40:11.970
I used to think short
term memory is like crazy,

00:40:11.971 --> 00:40:13.080
why is it like we can

00:40:13.081 --> 00:40:15.375
only remember
seven things at a time?

00:40:15.376 --> 00:40:19.140
Our brain is so big, why
would we have this limitation?

00:40:19.141 --> 00:40:22.530
It's sounds like we

00:40:22.531 --> 00:40:25.510
are under using
our computational capacity.

00:40:25.511 --> 00:40:30.100
Well, so I'm claiming that
this may actually be a

00:40:30.101 --> 00:40:33.695
prior that we use

00:40:33.696 --> 00:40:35.815
and that potentially machine
only could use,

00:40:35.816 --> 00:40:38.640
in order to constrain
representations.

00:40:38.641 --> 00:40:41.235
And the prior is that,

00:40:41.236 --> 00:40:44.650
there are, the assumption
about the world is that,

00:40:44.651 --> 00:40:46.880
there are many important things
that can be said about

00:40:46.881 --> 00:40:52.975
the world which can be
expressed in one sentence,

00:40:52.976 --> 00:40:56.480
which can be expressed by
a low dimensional statement.

00:40:56.481 --> 00:41:01.340
Which refer to
just a few variables.

00:41:01.341 --> 00:41:04.120
And often they
are discreet those

00:41:04.121 --> 00:41:06.110
we express in language,
sometimes they're not.

00:41:06.111 --> 00:41:10.130
We can like draw or somehow,

00:41:10.131 --> 00:41:14.445
use that to plan.

00:41:14.446 --> 00:41:17.900
But they are very,
very low dimensional.

00:41:17.901 --> 00:41:19.290
And it's not obvious,

00:41:19.291 --> 00:41:22.160
if priori that things about

00:41:22.161 --> 00:41:23.470
the world could be said that are

00:41:23.471 --> 00:41:25.795
true and low dimensional.

00:41:25.796 --> 00:41:29.635
So for example, again,
I'm using a prop.

00:41:29.636 --> 00:41:32.160
If I try to predict
the future here,

00:41:32.161 --> 00:41:33.560
there are many
aspects of it that

00:41:33.561 --> 00:41:35.010
are hard for me to predict.

00:41:35.011 --> 00:41:37.325
Like, where is it
going to land exactly?

00:41:37.326 --> 00:41:38.950
It's very, very hard to predict,

00:41:38.951 --> 00:41:40.650
right? It's a game.

00:41:40.651 --> 00:41:42.800
But, I could predict that
it's going to be on the floor.

00:41:42.801 --> 00:41:44.350
It's one bit of information.

00:41:44.351 --> 00:41:46.290
And I can predict
that with very,

00:41:46.291 --> 00:41:49.030
very high certainty
and a lot of what

00:41:49.031 --> 00:41:52.520
we talk about are
these kinds of statements.

00:41:52.521 --> 00:41:54.700
Like, if I drop the object,

00:41:54.701 --> 00:41:58.575
it's going to end on
the floor, in this context.

00:41:58.576 --> 00:42:02.880
So, this is the assumption
that we're trying

00:42:02.881 --> 00:42:07.325
to encapsulate in
machine learning terms

00:42:07.326 --> 00:42:10.485
with this consciousness
prior idea.

00:42:10.486 --> 00:42:19.820
So, one part of it is that
we need a mechanism that's

00:42:19.821 --> 00:42:23.620
going to select
a few relevant variables

00:42:23.621 --> 00:42:25.840
from all of the things that we

00:42:25.841 --> 00:42:28.460
could have access to
with our consciousness.

00:42:28.461 --> 00:42:32.810
So, everything that
we can think we see,

00:42:32.811 --> 00:42:35.115
everything that
we can talk about,

00:42:35.116 --> 00:42:38.000
are the things that
we have access to from

00:42:38.001 --> 00:42:40.680
low level perception to
very abstract things,

00:42:40.681 --> 00:42:42.240
explaining what we're seeing.

00:42:42.241 --> 00:42:44.170
We can come to
our consciousness.

00:42:44.171 --> 00:42:47.080
So, we need an
attention mechanism,

00:42:47.081 --> 00:42:48.480
in order to just pick

00:42:48.481 --> 00:42:52.395
a few things that are going
to go to our attention,

00:42:52.396 --> 00:42:54.740
go to our consciousness and

00:42:54.741 --> 00:42:58.220
that's the attention mechanism.

00:42:58.221 --> 00:43:08.320
So, we're working
with soft attention,

00:43:08.321 --> 00:43:09.770
soft cotton based attention,

00:43:09.771 --> 00:43:10.940
which is precisely the kind of

00:43:10.941 --> 00:43:12.640
attention mechanism
that we introduced for

00:43:12.641 --> 00:43:14.530
machine translation
a few years ago as Susan was

00:43:14.531 --> 00:43:17.115
talking about and has
been amazingly successful,

00:43:17.116 --> 00:43:19.960
not just from
Machine Translation,

00:43:19.961 --> 00:43:22.885
but for all kinds
of applications now.

00:43:22.886 --> 00:43:27.915
And I think we could use
the same kind of mechanisms.

00:43:27.916 --> 00:43:32.880
And so, I've been using
the word consciousness,

00:43:32.881 --> 00:43:35.440
but the word consciousness
is loaded with

00:43:35.441 --> 00:43:36.760
all kinds of meanings
and we have to

00:43:36.761 --> 00:43:38.960
be careful here
about what we mean.

00:43:38.961 --> 00:43:42.160
So different psychologists or

00:43:42.161 --> 00:43:45.565
philosophers are using terms
like access consciousness

00:43:45.566 --> 00:43:52.200
or the Heyns calls it
global availability,

00:43:52.201 --> 00:43:57.380
but basically it's
just the aspect

00:43:57.381 --> 00:43:59.650
of consciousness that concerns

00:43:59.651 --> 00:44:03.770
the selection of elements on

00:44:03.771 --> 00:44:07.630
which we are focusing in
order to make a prediction,

00:44:07.631 --> 00:44:13.580
in order to act and that we
can usually report verbally.

00:44:14.270 --> 00:44:20.140
So, how does the content base

00:44:20.141 --> 00:44:24.665
attention work in
machine translation?

00:44:24.666 --> 00:44:32.140
We use that to select
representations,

00:44:32.141 --> 00:44:35.290
a different position
in a sequence,

00:44:35.291 --> 00:44:36.920
an input sequence
that needed to be

00:44:36.921 --> 00:44:39.110
translated so that when we

00:44:39.111 --> 00:44:42.900
decide on the next word
in on an R and N,

00:44:42.901 --> 00:44:44.310
that predicts the next word in

00:44:44.311 --> 00:44:47.350
a sequence for
the translated sentence.

00:44:47.351 --> 00:44:50.910
We can focus on
one or a few words,

00:44:50.911 --> 00:44:52.160
which are likely to contain

00:44:52.161 --> 00:44:53.750
the most relevant information

00:44:53.751 --> 00:44:55.955
for the next word to produce.

00:44:55.956 --> 00:44:57.850
And we use an
attention mechanism,

00:44:57.851 --> 00:44:59.965
you could think of it
like little Neutral Net,

00:44:59.966 --> 00:45:03.720
that takes two things and input.

00:45:03.721 --> 00:45:07.165
It takes one candidate location,

00:45:07.166 --> 00:45:09.700
so that where we want to focus,

00:45:09.701 --> 00:45:10.860
there are some features
that are being

00:45:10.861 --> 00:45:15.685
computed and it takes
the current state,

00:45:15.686 --> 00:45:17.570
which is sort of the context,

00:45:17.571 --> 00:45:18.900
in which we are going
to take the decision

00:45:18.901 --> 00:45:20.535
of where to focus.

00:45:20.536 --> 00:45:23.170
It outputs a score that says,

00:45:23.171 --> 00:45:25.740
how much we want to focus
our attention at this location.

00:45:25.741 --> 00:45:27.100
And we're going to
compute such a score

00:45:27.101 --> 00:45:29.105
for all the possible locations.

00:45:29.106 --> 00:45:31.170
And then you can generalize

00:45:31.171 --> 00:45:33.910
this idea in all kinds
of ways but this

00:45:33.911 --> 00:45:36.265
is the heart of

00:45:36.266 --> 00:45:39.530
what we're doing with
content based attention.

00:45:39.531 --> 00:45:42.790
It's been used to greatly reduce

00:45:42.791 --> 00:45:47.735
the gap between
classical machine translation,

00:45:47.736 --> 00:45:49.720
based on engrams and

00:45:49.721 --> 00:45:55.635
human quality translation at

00:45:55.636 --> 00:45:57.955
least according to
human evaluation,

00:45:57.956 --> 00:46:01.995
a statistical method
to evaluate quality.

00:46:01.996 --> 00:46:05.810
So going back to
the consciousness Prior.

00:46:05.811 --> 00:46:10.095
Whereas in traditional
Machine Learning

00:46:10.096 --> 00:46:11.460
and Representation Learning,

00:46:11.461 --> 00:46:13.960
we think of this top
level presentation

00:46:13.961 --> 00:46:15.995
that captures all of

00:46:15.996 --> 00:46:18.005
the factors of interests

00:46:18.006 --> 00:46:22.210
here we're going to have
two levels of representation.

00:46:22.211 --> 00:46:25.340
We're going to have this

00:46:25.341 --> 00:46:28.255
very high dimensional
and conscious state,

00:46:28.256 --> 00:46:31.875
which contains all of
the abstract representations,

00:46:31.876 --> 00:46:35.180
but it contains everything

00:46:35.181 --> 00:46:37.870
and not just the ones that
are coming to our mind.

00:46:37.871 --> 00:46:40.010
Not just the ones that are
coming to our attention.

00:46:40.011 --> 00:46:41.520
The ones that we
are focusing on at

00:46:41.521 --> 00:46:43.980
a particular time
will be somehow

00:46:43.981 --> 00:46:47.720
stored in this Low-dimensional

00:46:47.721 --> 00:46:49.695
representation,
the conscious state.

00:46:49.696 --> 00:46:51.830
And just like in
the previous slide,

00:46:51.831 --> 00:46:53.600
we are going to have
an attention mechanism,

00:46:53.601 --> 00:46:55.950
which decide what to

00:46:55.951 --> 00:46:59.280
pick next to go into
the conscious state.

00:46:59.281 --> 00:47:02.380
And you can think of it like,

00:47:02.381 --> 00:47:04.660
it's kind of a choose

00:47:04.661 --> 00:47:09.635
something out of
this soup of information.

00:47:09.636 --> 00:47:13.220
And presumably, they are
all going to be recurrent,

00:47:13.221 --> 00:47:15.875
because everything
is happening in time.

00:47:15.876 --> 00:47:19.350
And an important
element of this is,

00:47:19.351 --> 00:47:22.020
the reason we're doing all this,

00:47:22.021 --> 00:47:24.365
is to put pressure

00:47:24.366 --> 00:47:27.940
on the mapping between
input and representations,

00:47:27.941 --> 00:47:29.280
unconscious
representation which is

00:47:29.281 --> 00:47:31.010
like the N quarter I had before.

00:47:31.011 --> 00:47:34.155
So that the N quarter would
learn representations,

00:47:34.156 --> 00:47:35.985
that have the property,

00:47:35.986 --> 00:47:40.775
that if I pick
just a few elements of it,

00:47:40.776 --> 00:47:43.130
I can make a true statement or

00:47:43.131 --> 00:47:45.410
very highly probable statement
about the world,

00:47:45.411 --> 00:47:48.480
maybe a highly
probable prediction.

00:47:48.481 --> 00:47:51.220
So yeah, the name,

00:47:51.221 --> 00:47:53.595
the objective here is the same
as I had at the beginning.

00:47:53.596 --> 00:47:55.360
So we are only
using all of this,

00:47:55.361 --> 00:47:58.755
to imposing this sort
of pressure,

00:47:58.756 --> 00:48:01.100
constraint on the
representation learner,

00:48:01.101 --> 00:48:02.630
so that it learns
representations that have

00:48:02.631 --> 00:48:05.025
this property that we can,

00:48:05.026 --> 00:48:08.265
this defines a language
in which we can

00:48:08.266 --> 00:48:11.690
say things compactly
that are true.

00:48:11.691 --> 00:48:13.540
And each of those things
we're saying are

00:48:13.541 --> 00:48:16.125
like just a few
variables at a time.

00:48:16.126 --> 00:48:18.400
Another interesting
thing that happens

00:48:18.401 --> 00:48:19.650
here and that connects to

00:48:19.651 --> 00:48:22.470
classical AI is that we

00:48:22.471 --> 00:48:28.080
now going to have
to represent here,

00:48:28.081 --> 00:48:36.970
not just values of variables
but names of variables.

00:48:36.971 --> 00:48:42.030
This is something unusual
for Neural Nets and you

00:48:42.031 --> 00:48:44.560
start seeing things like

00:48:44.561 --> 00:48:46.530
this and models like
neural training machine,

00:48:46.531 --> 00:48:49.650
where we have these memory
with keys and values.

00:48:49.651 --> 00:48:54.190
So, the reason we need to
have names of things is that,

00:48:54.191 --> 00:48:55.690
for example, if I
make a prediction

00:48:55.691 --> 00:48:57.160
about a future variable,

00:48:57.161 --> 00:48:58.710
then what I'm trying to do,

00:48:58.711 --> 00:49:01.430
what I have to story here is,

00:49:01.431 --> 00:49:04.640
the name of the variable on
which I'm making a prediction

00:49:04.641 --> 00:49:08.225
separate from its value
somehow, because later,

00:49:08.226 --> 00:49:10.250
I'd like to be able to say, "Oh,

00:49:10.251 --> 00:49:13.295
I had made a prediction
about this variable and

00:49:13.296 --> 00:49:15.215
here's the observed value

00:49:15.216 --> 00:49:16.960
and here was the
predict value and now,

00:49:16.961 --> 00:49:19.660
I'm going to have to
update my parameters."

00:49:19.661 --> 00:49:22.830
And so, if I were to mush

00:49:22.831 --> 00:49:25.810
the names and the values, I
wouldn't be able to do that.

00:49:25.811 --> 00:49:29.145
I need to be able to refer
to things indirectly.

00:49:29.146 --> 00:49:31.400
We can do that with Neural Nets,

00:49:31.401 --> 00:49:36.360
but we just have to design
them with that in mind.

00:49:37.250 --> 00:49:46.040
So one little scenario that one

00:49:46.041 --> 00:49:49.850
can look at is using

00:49:49.851 --> 00:49:52.440
the conscious state to

00:49:52.441 --> 00:49:56.625
make a prediction about
a specific variable,

00:49:56.626 --> 00:49:58.560
using only of course

00:49:58.561 --> 00:50:01.855
a few variables that are
part of the conscious state.

00:50:01.856 --> 00:50:05.930
And then we could

00:50:05.931 --> 00:50:07.510
just use a kind of
log like you would

00:50:07.511 --> 00:50:09.595
which tells us how
good is our prediction,

00:50:09.596 --> 00:50:12.610
weighted by on which variable
we're making prediction,

00:50:12.611 --> 00:50:14.080
assuming we only
predict one thing at

00:50:14.081 --> 00:50:15.995
a time in this very
simple scenario.

00:50:15.996 --> 00:50:18.400
But just making

00:50:18.401 --> 00:50:22.910
this weighted prediction
isn't going to be enough,

00:50:22.911 --> 00:50:25.515
otherwise, the system would just

00:50:25.516 --> 00:50:28.700
learn to extract variables
that are easy to predict,

00:50:28.701 --> 00:50:31.010
but are kind of
meaningless or useless.

00:50:31.011 --> 00:50:33.605
And there are
many such variables.

00:50:33.606 --> 00:50:37.000
So, we are currently

00:50:37.001 --> 00:50:39.600
exploring different training
objectives for this.

00:50:39.601 --> 00:50:42.580
But one important idea is,

00:50:42.581 --> 00:50:47.730
would like the representations
to have high entropy,

00:50:47.731 --> 00:50:49.680
in other words to
preserve a lot of

00:50:49.681 --> 00:50:52.500
information about the data.

00:50:52.501 --> 00:50:54.570
So that's the idea of maximizing

00:50:54.571 --> 00:50:56.115
entropy of the representation.

00:50:56.116 --> 00:50:57.850
There's also a very old idea

00:50:57.851 --> 00:50:59.500
which we're going to be reusing,

00:50:59.501 --> 00:51:01.530
which is the idea of maximizing

00:51:01.531 --> 00:51:02.750
mutual information between

00:51:02.751 --> 00:51:04.670
past and future representations.

00:51:04.671 --> 00:51:07.340
So Sue Becker who did her

00:51:07.341 --> 00:51:12.175
Ph.D. with Geoff Hinton
and by the same time as me,

00:51:12.176 --> 00:51:16.090
use these kinds of criteria to

00:51:16.091 --> 00:51:20.090
extract features in
the spatial domain,

00:51:20.091 --> 00:51:21.410
that had the property that there

00:51:21.411 --> 00:51:22.890
was high mutual
information between

00:51:22.891 --> 00:51:27.015
the value of the features
at nearby locations.

00:51:27.016 --> 00:51:30.935
And I think this is something
that's relevant here,

00:51:30.936 --> 00:51:32.730
for we're trying to do.

00:51:32.731 --> 00:51:34.200
We're trying to define

00:51:34.201 --> 00:51:37.640
a training objective for
this consciousness thing

00:51:37.641 --> 00:51:43.980
that makes past's
conscious states

00:51:43.981 --> 00:51:47.355
highly predictive of
future conscious states.

00:51:47.356 --> 00:51:50.470
But not just predictive,

00:51:50.471 --> 00:51:52.890
but also high
mutual information.

00:51:52.891 --> 00:51:54.500
So in other words, that they

00:51:54.501 --> 00:51:57.425
also capture a lot of
information together.

00:51:57.426 --> 00:52:02.635
Okay. Another
potential source of

00:52:02.636 --> 00:52:04.720
training objective
which I would like to

00:52:04.721 --> 00:52:07.880
minimize as much as possible
but maybe we have no choice,

00:52:07.881 --> 00:52:10.910
is not just a kind
of predictability,

00:52:10.911 --> 00:52:14.630
but a sort of usefulness
for enforcement lining.

00:52:14.631 --> 00:52:20.265
And so, what do we
use our thoughts for?

00:52:20.266 --> 00:52:22.840
Well, it's very clear that

00:52:22.841 --> 00:52:26.875
our thoughts have a very strong
influence on our actions.

00:52:26.876 --> 00:52:30.090
So our thoughts are used
to condition our actions

00:52:30.091 --> 00:52:33.160
and also plan our actions.

00:52:33.161 --> 00:52:34.530
So very often we have

00:52:34.531 --> 00:52:37.505
mental imagery and right
after we do something,

00:52:37.506 --> 00:52:38.820
because it helps us figure

00:52:38.821 --> 00:52:40.370
out whether it's
a good thing to do or not.

00:52:40.371 --> 00:52:42.950
Right? So we could use

00:52:42.951 --> 00:52:45.740
this consciousness
mechanism just

00:52:45.741 --> 00:52:47.975
conditioning
information for policy,

00:52:47.976 --> 00:52:56.500
and allow this not just to
make a single prediction,

00:52:56.501 --> 00:53:00.990
but, imagine a future

00:53:00.991 --> 00:53:05.540
with unfolding of
many time steps in the future.

00:53:05.541 --> 00:53:09.560
A few more things
I want to close on.

00:53:09.561 --> 00:53:13.420
I mentioned that we

00:53:13.421 --> 00:53:14.620
don't really want to

00:53:14.621 --> 00:53:17.090
have like a different neuron
for each factor,

00:53:17.091 --> 00:53:20.500
we want to use this notion

00:53:20.501 --> 00:53:23.790
of a district representation
for each factor.

00:53:23.791 --> 00:53:25.670
So you can think
of each factor as

00:53:25.671 --> 00:53:28.430
like a concept that we
can use language for,

00:53:28.431 --> 00:53:30.470
and so they're going
to have an embedding.

00:53:30.471 --> 00:53:33.180
And there's been actually

00:53:33.181 --> 00:53:36.410
some earlier work done
by Mike Moser in the 90s

00:53:36.411 --> 00:53:40.330
about how one could

00:53:40.331 --> 00:53:46.550
represent discrete concepts
in a neural net,

00:53:46.551 --> 00:53:49.280
using something
like Help field net.

00:53:49.281 --> 00:53:53.135
So if you imagine that you
have a group of neurons

00:53:53.136 --> 00:53:58.060
that collaborate with each

00:53:58.061 --> 00:54:00.230
other to move together

00:54:00.231 --> 00:54:04.670
towards a sort of stable
fixed point stable attractor,

00:54:04.671 --> 00:54:07.165
that sort of clean up mechanism,

00:54:07.166 --> 00:54:08.560
it is a sort of

00:54:08.561 --> 00:54:11.550
discretization that makes a lot

00:54:11.551 --> 00:54:14.490
of sense from base on
psychological experiments,

00:54:14.491 --> 00:54:18.315
to explain our ability to

00:54:18.316 --> 00:54:20.890
manipulate discrete concepts in

00:54:20.891 --> 00:54:24.715
our mind, and take decisions.

00:54:24.716 --> 00:54:30.620
Right. There's of course

00:54:30.621 --> 00:54:32.780
connections between
this work and classical.

00:54:32.781 --> 00:54:34.835
Yeah. You can think of

00:54:34.836 --> 00:54:38.510
something like a statement
about predicting a variable

00:54:38.511 --> 00:54:39.940
given conditioning variables as

00:54:39.941 --> 00:54:42.080
just a connection is

00:54:42.081 --> 00:54:45.180
the way of talking about
a classical AI rule.

00:54:45.181 --> 00:54:47.770
Right? It doesn't have to be
a rule, it could be a fact,

00:54:47.771 --> 00:54:48.880
it could be something about

00:54:48.881 --> 00:54:51.645
the current scene
that we know is true,

00:54:51.646 --> 00:54:53.430
or has a high
probability that is also

00:54:53.431 --> 00:54:56.815
represented in
the conscious thought.

00:54:56.816 --> 00:55:01.100
I think that this notion
of having to refer to

00:55:01.101 --> 00:55:05.305
variables by the representation
of the variables itself,

00:55:05.306 --> 00:55:08.160
the symbol has a name,

00:55:08.161 --> 00:55:10.010
is something that comes

00:55:10.011 --> 00:55:12.675
handy if you want to
implement some kind of

00:55:12.676 --> 00:55:17.640
recursive compositional
computation which

00:55:17.641 --> 00:55:20.475
is very common in classical AI.

00:55:20.476 --> 00:55:24.640
And of course, this also
makes connection to language.

00:55:24.641 --> 00:55:28.810
I'm hoping that it's
going to help to

00:55:28.811 --> 00:55:33.550
associate perceptual words
with national language,

00:55:33.551 --> 00:55:37.465
to ground the actual language,
and vice versa.

00:55:37.466 --> 00:55:39.945
What I'm hoping is that

00:55:39.946 --> 00:55:44.205
when learners that have this
kind of consciousness prior,

00:55:44.206 --> 00:55:48.330
are learning with language and

00:55:48.331 --> 00:55:52.850
perception that
the natural language

00:55:52.851 --> 00:55:54.945
they're getting from
humans is going to help

00:55:54.946 --> 00:55:58.410
the learners by giving

00:55:58.411 --> 00:56:01.130
them hints about
the high level abstractions.

00:56:01.131 --> 00:56:03.350
So I think when we
talk to our children,

00:56:03.351 --> 00:56:05.430
we're giving them
hints about what are

00:56:05.431 --> 00:56:06.780
relevant abstractions that are

00:56:06.781 --> 00:56:09.175
useful in the world around them,

00:56:09.176 --> 00:56:13.580
and probably accelerate
their learning in this way.

00:56:13.581 --> 00:56:17.120
Okay. I see that
it's already 4:00.

00:56:17.121 --> 00:56:22.080
Let me have a last slide

00:56:22.081 --> 00:56:25.065
here about something
very different.

00:56:25.066 --> 00:56:30.440
I think it's time
the machine learning community,

00:56:30.441 --> 00:56:32.790
research community
starts thinking

00:56:32.791 --> 00:56:35.815
beyond developing
the next gadget.

00:56:35.816 --> 00:56:39.920
Of course those gadgets
are very profitable,

00:56:39.921 --> 00:56:43.290
but there are other things
that matter in the world.

00:56:43.291 --> 00:56:48.190
And it'd be nice if grad
students and researchers around

00:56:48.191 --> 00:56:50.450
the world instead of
working see an image net

00:56:50.451 --> 00:56:53.270
could work on datasets,

00:56:53.271 --> 00:56:57.720
that if we could have good
solutions for those datasets,

00:56:57.721 --> 00:57:02.200
could help millions of
people with medical problems,

00:57:02.201 --> 00:57:08.275
